-- title:
--   A standard loop projects to a half-twist word
-- statement:
--   The image of a standard loop is a product of elementary half-twists.
--
--   Precisely: take the $j$-th standard loop of the plane punctured at $n$ points, include it as the last strand of a configuration of $n+1$ points, and project to unordered configurations. The resulting braid lies in the subgroup generated by the elementary half-twists.
--
--   This is the classical identity exhibiting a pure braid generator as a half-twist word,
--
--   $$A_{i,n+1} \;=\; \bigl(\sigma_n \sigma_{n-1} \cdots \sigma_{i+1}\bigr)\, \sigma_i^{2} \, \bigl(\sigma_{i+1}^{-1} \cdots \sigma_{n-1}^{-1} \sigma_n^{-1}\bigr),$$
--
--   read geometrically. The standard loop runs the last strand out along a parabola, once around the $j$-th puncture, and back. As a braid this is: carry the last strand leftwards past the strands lying between it and the $j$-th, which is the word $\sigma_n \cdots \sigma_{j+1}$; perform a full twist $\sigma_j^{2}$ with the $j$-th strand, which is the encircling; then carry it back, which is the inverse word.
--
--   Everything around this statement is now discharged. The inductive step for generation is proved from it together with the combing skeleton and the fact that adding a far-right strand sends half-twists to half-twists; the reduction of that step to this statement goes through the Fadell--Neuwirth identification of the kernel with the fundamental group of the punctured plane and the fact that the standard loops generate that group.
--
--   Two remarks on why it is stated this way.
--
--   It is stated for the *image* of a standard loop rather than for an abstractly given generator, because the generators must be named for the statement to have content: an unspecified free basis of $\pi_1(E^2-Q_n)$ carries no information about which braid its elements become. The loops are constructed explicitly in `Def_BraidsLinksMCG_StandardLoops` for exactly this reason.
--
--   It asks only for membership in the half-twist subgroup, not for the explicit word above. A proof may produce whatever word is convenient; the displayed identity is the classical choice, and the geometric reading is what a formal proof would have to reproduce, most directly by exhibiting a homotopy between the projected standard loop and the corresponding concatenation of half-twist loops.
-- source:
--   Birman, Braids, Links and Mapping Class Groups, Chapter 1, the pure braid generators A_{ij}; Tarcha, Braid Theory and the Artin Presentation, Teorema 3.11.

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BraidsLinksMCG_ConfigSpace
import Definitions.Def_BraidsLinksMCG_StandardLoops
import Definitions.Def_TarchaBraids_HalfTwist

namespace BraidsLinksMCG

open TarchaBraids

theorem standardGen_image_mem_halfTwist (n : ℕ) (j : Fin n) :
    (FundamentalGroup.map (configProj (n + 1)) (baseOrdered (n + 1)))
        ((FundamentalGroup.mapOfEq (configIncl n) (configIncl_base n)) (standardGen n j))
      ∈ Subgroup.closure
          (Set.range (fun i : Fin (n + 1 - 1) => halfTwistBraid (n + 1) i)) := by sorry

end BraidsLinksMCG
